WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.103
BEN AARON: In the 1800s, as
engineers searched for cost

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.438
effective alternatives
to horses and steam

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.674
for powering public
transport, wind,

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:10.977
in the form of large kites,
were seriously considered.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.179
But now after two
centuries of development,

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:15.148
we still don't have
kite powered buses.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:20.787
But we can do this.

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.455
The board flip.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:25.358
Yes, the butter slide.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.795
A nice fence for a grind.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:31.664
Watch out for my post turn.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:34.200
And then there's this.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:35.468
The roll.

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.837
An airborne inverted spin.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:40.240
Nice work.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.176
See you next time.

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.678
That was an advanced
version of a role.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.848
A core kite surfing trick
with plenty of variations.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:53.820
Kite surfers can front roll,
back roll, roll, and nose grab.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.755
There are all sorts of
ways of mixing it up.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:03.963
And that wasn't one of them.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.233
To perfect the right kind of
airborne rotation for a kite

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.104
surfing role requires a mastery
of angular momentum and lift

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:12.105
force.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:12.972
Here's the roll 101.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:18.611
As wind flows over a
kite's wing-like shape,

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.181
it generates lift force,
pulling him across the water.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.219
On launch, he throws
his body backwards,

00:01:26.286 --> 00:01:28.822
generating just enough
angular momentum

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:29.722
for a complete rotation.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:35.628
To land, he steers his kite
downwards and ahead of him.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.765
This increases lift force
in the direction of travel,

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:39.866
so he avoids sinking.

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:45.438
It is complicated stuff
to deal with all at once,

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.740
so first, let's just
practice using lift

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.409
force to get a little air.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.283
OK, We've got that covered.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.519
Now let's add some angular
momentum for the roll.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.558
I said, some.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.026
I mean, he did
get enough angular

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.496
momentum for one rotation.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:10.763
But he didn't have time for two.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.200
OK.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.502
Let's work on the landing.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.670
Now, can you remember
which direction

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.172
we need to send that kite?

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.779
No, quite the opposite.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.948
He actually aced the
angular momentum,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.151
But if you want to
go in this direction,

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.621
like your board,
and your kite is

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.789
steered down in this direction,

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:40.593
It's not going to happen.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.394
OK.

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:42.161
Last chance.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.135
Arguably worse.

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:50.904
Lift force in wrong direction,

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:52.839
Too much angular momentum.

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:56.576
Very sinky landing.

